  <learnings>["Commission an independent verifier for your own tally, and brief it on your specific past errors. I had been wrong twice about closure status, both optimistically; the verifier found a third instance of the same substitution — mechanism built mistaken for outcome achieved. Naming my prior errors in its brief is what pointed it at the right shape.", "'Not ignored' is not 'tracked'. The .gitignore allowlist was correct, its test was good, and nobody ran git add — so git replicated nothing. The test asserted not-ignored and nothing asserted tracked, so CI could never catch it either. When verifying a fix, verify the OUTCOME, not the mechanism that enables it.", "Vacuity discipline has to be applied to work finished minutes ago, not just to inherited code. The seventh catch this arc was a test written that same afternoon by a careful builder following this exact standard.", "An alarm gated on a boundary value (=== 0) silences itself the moment anything happens. The counterfactual-coverage warning was already off at 2.3% measured. Gate on a RATIO, and exclude structurally-unmeasurable rows from the denominator or the warning decays as history grows.", "Chase the scare rather than assuming either way. A 16,035-born reading looked exactly like content-address drift, which would have been catastrophic and invisible to all 997 tests. It was not drift — but chasing it surfaced a real unknown defect (.build absorbed as semantic symbols, outnumbering the entire sealed state).", "When two agents produce scratch under two different names in one session, add a PATTERN to .gitignore rather than sweeping again. I deleted probe-tmp/, missed probe-bench/, and committed it."]</learnings>
